C = [
("h1", "4. Hosea and Micah: Mercy, and Not Sacrifice"),
("p", "Claim: Hosea 6:6 and Micah 6:6–8 are the two sentences most often read as the prophets’ rejection of sacrifice, and each is a statement of rank inside a charge against the worshipers. Hosea’s verse is built as a pair of lines that interpret each other, and the second line says “more than.” Micah’s verse answers a worshiper who asks how much he must bring by naming what the LORD has already shown, and the verses after it return to false weights and deceit. Neither prophet proposes that the offerings cease. Hosea lists the loss of sacrifice among the judgments, and Micah asks for justice, mercy and humility as the walk of a man who comes."),
("sub", "4.1 Hosea’s controversy: no truth, nor mercy, nor knowledge of God"),
("p", "Hosea opens his indictment with a word that will return in chapter 6. “Hear the word of the LORD, ye children of Israel: for the LORD hath a controversy with the inhabitants of the land, because there is no truth, nor mercy, nor knowledge of God in the land” (Hosea 4:1). The three missing things are truth, mercy and the knowledge of God. The next verse lists the conduct: “By swearing, and lying, and killing, and stealing, and committing adultery, they break out, and blood toucheth blood” (Hosea 4:2). The LORD’s controversy is the same lawsuit Isaiah and Micah bring, and its first count is the absence of mercy and of the knowledge of God, the pair that stands again in Hosea 6:6."),
("p", "The priests are charged next. “My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge: because thou hast rejected knowledge, I will also reject thee, that thou shalt be no priest to me: seeing thou hast forgotten the law of thy God, I will also forget thy children” (Hosea 4:6). The prophet does not say the priesthood is wrong. He says this priest has rejected knowledge and forgotten the law and will be no priest to the LORD. The next verses speak of the priests in the same terms: “They eat up the sin of my people, and they set their heart on their iniquity” (Hosea 4:8). Many readers take the verse to mean that the priests profit from the sin offerings and so want the people to sin. The volume reads it so, with the note that the verse does not itself name the sin offering, and in either case it is a charge against the priests."),
("p", "The worship at the high places is charged separately. “They sacrifice upon the tops of the mountains, and burn incense upon the hills, under oaks and poplars and elms, because the shadow thereof is good” (Hosea 4:13). The reason the verse gives is that the shadow is good. The place is chosen for the worshipers’ comfort, and it is a place the LORD had not appointed. Hosea 5:6 adds a sentence that no later reading can soften: “They shall go with their flocks and with their herds to seek the LORD; but they shall not find him; he hath withdrawn himself from them.” The LORD is not found by the flocks and herds that are brought to seek him. The offerings are not what he is withholding."),
("sub", "4.2 Hosea 6:1–7: the sentence in its place"),
("p", "Chapter 6 opens with Israel’s own words of return. “Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up. After two days will he revive us: in the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight” (Hosea 6:1, 2). The LORD does not take the words as a turning. “O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away” (Hosea 6:4). The word the King James Version renders “goodness” in this verse is the same word it renders “mercy” in verse 6. The people’s goodness vanishes like the morning mist. What the LORD wants is a goodness that does not."),
("p", "Verse 6 follows. “For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings” (Hosea 6:6). The verse is two lines, and they are a pair. The first says mercy and not sacrifice. The second says the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings. The second line is the key to the first. It uses “more than,” the language of comparison, and so shows how the pair is to be read: mercy over sacrifice, the knowledge of God over burnt offerings. The same pairing of “not” and “more than” appears in the earlier voices (1 Samuel 15:22, “to obey is better than sacrifice”). The volume reads the verse as a statement of rank and not of abolition."),
("p", "Verse 7 states the reason. “But they like men have transgressed the covenant: there have they dealt treacherously against me” (Hosea 6:7). The offence is the broken covenant. The sacrifices are named as the thing mercy outranks; the covenant is named as the thing broken. Hosea 8:12 adds, “I have written to him the great things of my law, but they were counted as a strange thing.” The people have the law in writing and treat it as foreign."),
("sub", "4.3 My offerings, and the loss of sacrifice"),
("p", "Two further sentences in Hosea show what the LORD leaves standing. The first is in chapter 8. “Because Ephraim hath made many altars to sin, altars shall be unto him to sin. … They sacrifice flesh for the sacrifices of mine offerings, and eat it; but the LORD accepteth them not; now will he remember their iniquity, and visit their sins: they shall return to Egypt” (Hosea 8:11, 13). The LORD calls them “the sacrifices of mine offerings.” He does not disown the offerings, and he does not accept the worshipers. The charge is the making of many altars to sin, and the sentence is a return to Egypt."),
("p", "The second is in chapter 3, in the account of what the exile will take. “For the children of Israel shall abide many days without a king, and without a prince, and without a sacrifice, and without an image, and without an ephod, and without teraphim: afterward shall the children of Israel return, and seek the LORD their God, and David their king; and shall fear the LORD and his goodness in the latter days” (Hosea 3:4, 5). The list mixes things the LORD gave Israel and things he condemned, and the volume does not sort it. It reports one thing without comment: sacrifice is named among the losses, with king and prince, and the return that follows is a return to the LORD and to David their king. The prophet who says the LORD desired mercy and not sacrifice also counts the absence of sacrifice as part of the judgment."),
("p", "The last chapter gives the people their words. “O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God; for thou hast fallen by thine iniquity. Take with you words, and turn to the LORD: say unto him, Take away all iniquity, and receive us graciously: so will we render the calves of our lips” (Hosea 14:1, 2). The word “calves” is the offering, and the offering is lips: the praise of a pardoned people. Even here, the language is the language of sacrifice."),
("sub", "4.4 The words in Matthew"),
("p", "Matthew twice has the Lord quote Hosea 6:6. When the Pharisees ask why he eats with publicans and sinners, he answers, “go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance” (Matthew 9:13). Again, when the disciples pluck ears of corn on the sabbath, he says, “if ye had known what this meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice, ye would not have condemned the guiltless” (Matthew 12:7). In both, the Lord is speaking to Pharisees in Israel about how the Law is to be kept, and in both he sends them to Hosea. In the second, he has just said that “the priests in the temple profane the sabbath, and are blameless” (Matthew 12:5), and that “in this place is one greater than the temple” (Matthew 12:6). The Lord takes the temple service for granted as a service in which priests are blameless."),
("p", "The volume reports both quotations in their settings and argues neither. Study 78 reads Matthew 12 for the Sabbath and Typology Commentary Volume 5 reports it in its treatment of the kingdom. For the present argument the point is small and sufficient: in both places the Lord quotes the verse the way the prophet used it, as a rule of rank, and he does not use it to end the temple service."),
("sub", "4.5 Micah’s lawsuit"),
("p", "Micah’s sixth chapter is another controversy. “Hear ye, O mountains, the LORD’s controversy, and ye strong foundations of the earth: for the LORD hath a controversy with his people, and he will plead with Israel” (Micah 6:2). The LORD’s first words are a question to the people: “O my people, what have I done unto thee? and wherein have I wearied thee? testify against me” (Micah 6:3). He recites what he did: “I brought thee up out of the land of Egypt, and redeemed thee out of the house of servants; and I sent before thee Moses, Aaron, and Miriam” (Micah 6:4). The case rests on the LORD’s faithfulness, and the people are asked to remember Balak and Balaam, “that ye may know the righteousness of the LORD” (Micah 6:5)."),
("p", "Then the worshiper speaks. “Wherewith shall I come before the LORD, and bow myself before the high God? shall I come before him with burnt offerings, with calves of a year old? Will the LORD be pleased with thousands of rams, or with ten thousands of rivers of oil? shall I give my firstborn for my transgression, the fruit of my body for the sin of my soul?” (Micah 6:6, 7). The questions climb: a burnt offering, a calf of a year old, thousands of rams, ten thousands of rivers of oil, a firstborn. The worshiper asks how much will be enough. The question assumes that the offering is a payment sized to the offence, and that more of it will purchase more."),
("p", "The prophet’s answer does not say the offerings should stop. “He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?” (Micah 6:8). The answer is that the LORD has already shown what is good, and that what he requires is three things in the walk of a man. The verse is silent about the offerings. It does not say they are not required. It says that the thing the worshiper is asking for, the price, is not in the account. The walk is. The verses after it return to the city: “Are there yet the treasures of wickedness in the house of the wicked, and the scant measure that is abominable? Shall I count them pure with the wicked balances, and with the bag of deceitful weights?” (Micah 6:10, 11). The answer of verse 8 is followed at once by the false weights, and the offerings of verses 6 and 7 are the thing meant to cover them."),
("ctbl", "Hosea and Micah in five passages.", [
    ("Passage", "What is said against the offering", "What is left standing"),
    ("Hosea 4:1–13", "No mercy nor knowledge of God; priests who have rejected knowledge; sacrifice on the hills for the shadow", "The priesthood is charged, not abolished (4:6); the high places are charged, not the house"),
    ("Hosea 5:6", "They go with flocks and herds to seek the LORD, and do not find him", "The LORD has withdrawn himself; the offerings are not what he withholds"),
    ("Hosea 6:6", "Mercy, and not sacrifice; the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings", "A pair of lines; the second says “more than”; the reason is the broken covenant (6:7)"),
    ("Hosea 8:11–13", "Many altars to sin; “the sacrifices of mine offerings” are not accepted", "The offerings are named as the LORD’s; the people are not accepted"),
    ("Hosea 3:4, 5; 14:2", "Many days without a king, a prince, a sacrifice", "Afterward they return and seek the LORD and David their king; “the calves of our lips”"),
    ("Micah 6:6–8", "The price of offerings cannot be set by the thousands of rams or the firstborn", "What the LORD requires is to do justly, love mercy and walk humbly; the verses that follow return to false weights"),
  ], [0.18, 0.41, 0.41]),
("sub", "4.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Hosea 6:6 is two lines; the second, “the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings,” shows that the first is a statement of rank.",
    "The first count of the LORD’s controversy in Hosea 4:1 is the absence of mercy and of the knowledge of God, and the reason of Hosea 6:7 is the broken covenant.",
    "The LORD calls the offerings that he does not accept “the sacrifices of mine offerings” (Hosea 8:13), and the loss of sacrifice is listed among the judgments (Hosea 3:4).",
    "Micah 6:6–8 answers a worshiper who asks how much to bring by naming what the LORD has shown, and the verses that follow return to false weights.",
    "Matthew 9:13 and 12:7 are reported in their settings as the Lord’s use of Hosea’s sentence in Israel, and the volume builds nothing on them beyond that.",
  ]),
]
